# SIN(3M)

## NAME

sin, cos, tan, asin, acos, atan, atan2 - trigonometric functions

## SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <math.h>

double sin(double x);

double cos(double x);

double tan(double x);

double asin(double x);

double acos(double x);

double atan(double x);

double atan2(double y, double x);
```

## DESCRIPTION

*Sin, cos* and *tan* return trigonometric functions of radian arguments. The magnitude of the
argument should be checked by the caller to make sure the result is meaningful.

*Asin* returns the arc sin in the range -π/2 to π/2.

*Acos* returns the arc cosine in the range 0 to π.

*Atan* returns the arc tangent of *x* in the range -π/2 to π/2.

*Atan2* returns the arc tangent of *y/x* in the range -π to π. **Note:** v7's page named the
arguments the other way round and called the result the arc tangent of *x/y*. The routine is
the same one; the names here are C11's, and <math.h> declares it **atan2(double y, double x)**.

## SEE ALSO

exp(3), sinh(3), intro(2)

## DIAGNOSTICS

Arguments of magnitude greater than 1 cause *asin* and *acos* to return value 0; *errno* is set
to EDOM. The value of *tan* at its singular points is HUGE_VAL with the sign of the pole, and
*errno* is set to ERANGE. **Note:** that is the "would have been an infinity" case, for which
this machine has no value; HUGE_VAL is the largest finite one.

Nothing on this page can overflow. *Atan* and *atan2* evaluate their fit on an argument reduced
to [-0.414, 0.414], and *asin* and *acos* reduce to *atan* after their range check, so no error
return but the two above exists.

## BUGS

The value of *tan* for arguments greater than about 2**40 is garbage. **Note:** v7 said 2**31,
which was its own mantissa and integer widths speaking. Here the reduction carries the integer
part of x/π through an **int**, which holds 41 bits, and above 2**40 a **double** has no
fractional bits left in any case.

## BESM-6 NOTES

### The reduction crossover was a width, not a bound

v7's *sin* reads **if (x > 32764)**, and that number is not mathematical: it is the last
multiple of the quarter-turn below 2**15, past which the PDP-11's **int** overflowed, so above
it v7 fell back on a slower reduction through modf(3).

Here **int** is 41 bits, and the real point past which the fast integer reduction loses bits is
2**40 - the width of the mantissa, and the same constant *modf* itself keys on. The crossover
moves out to 2**40 accordingly, so the fast path serves the whole range a program is likely to
ask for. A magnitude past 2**40 has no fractional turn left to speak of anyway.

### atan needed one line of C11

v7's first line of *atan.c* reads **double static sq2p1**, which this front end rejects: the
storage class has to precede the type. The routine is otherwise unchanged, the closing **if ((y
\+ x) == y)** test of *atan2* included - that is v7's way of asking whether the angle is a right
angle, and it works here for the same reason it worked on the PDP-11.

